rsz: give up on a non-WNS endpoint after twenty dry passes, not fifty

Policy candidate from the repair-knobs campaign (ideas/repair-knobs-nihl.md,
third study). Not upstreamed; carried while it is measured.

The legacy sweep lets every endpoint run fifty non-improving passes
before it restores the last checkpoint and moves on. The per-visit trace
of the default (patches/0078) on the study's designs says what those
passes buy: a paying endpoint's next improvement arrives within a few
passes of the previous one, and the fifty-pass tail is where the dry
passes go -- jpeg cts spends a quarter of its passes on eight visits
that improve, then grind fifty dry passes, then restore. The endpoint
that carries WNS is the exception: its first gain can come late
(riscv32i grt, pass 31), and it keeps the legacy patience.

One constant changes, for endpoints that do not carry WNS: twenty dry
passes, the smallest value that keeps 98% or more of the TNS gain on
every traced step (mock-alu's visits need five; jpeg's grt visits gain
again after dry gaps of ten to twenty). The order of the sweep, the fix-rate fence, the last-gasp phase
and every knob are untouched. Chesterton's Fence: the fifty was there for
late gains; the trace shows where late gains actually occur, and the
patch keeps the patience exactly there.

